# Target C — audited §170 fully correlated sparse-return exclusion through `sigma=0.0172`

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** audited-working proof-only full-core Type-II route theorem; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**  
**Dependencies:** the hostile-audited Section-117/118 sparse-directional signed-return mechanism and the geometry already certified through §167.

The previous sparse-return proof bounded its directional product and sparse penalty by separate maxima. Those maxima spend the same slack in incompatible coordinates. Keeping the complete slack simplex correlated produces a much larger safe exclusion window:

\[
\boxed{0\le\sigma\le s_0:=0.0172.}
\tag{170.1}
\]

The first numerical zero of the same fully correlated scalar mechanism is near `0.0173116294`; that value is not promoted. At `0.0172` the exact reduced return bracket remains above `0.00232`.

Use

\[
x+y+z+w=\sigma,
\qquad x,y,z,w\ge0,
\tag{170.2}
\]

and the signed-return necessity

\[
\lambda z\ge\kappa[J]_+,
\qquad
\kappa=2-\sqrt3.
\tag{170.3}
\]

The inherited return bracket is

\[
\begin{aligned}
J={}&A_*(x,w)
-\lambda\left(10z+{x\over2}+{25w\over3}\right)\\
&-2(U-x-y+2z)
\left(Q(y)+y+x+{10w\over3}\right),
\end{aligned}
\tag{170.4}
\]

where

\[
A_*(x,w)
={\pi\over4}-G(\theta)-2G(\phi_8+r)-G(r),
\qquad
r=x+{10w\over3},
\tag{170.5}
\]

\[
G(t)={t-\sin t\over8},
\]

and `Q(y)` is the increasing tooth inverse determined by

\[
\sin Q+\sqrt3(1-\cos Q)-Q={y\over2}.
\tag{170.6}
\]

Put

\[
\widetilde J=J-{\lambda z\over\kappa}.
\tag{170.7}
\]

If `widetilde J>0`, then (170.3) implies `lambda z>lambda z`, impossible. Thus it suffices to prove a positive uniform lower bound for `widetilde J` on the slack simplex.

---

## 170.1 Conservative endpoint relaxation

Let `theta_0` be the unique solution of

\[
3\theta_0-2\pi-\sin(\theta_0/2)-2\sin\theta_0=s_0.
\]

At `s_0=0.0172`,

\[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\theta_0&=2.70656462926518\ldots,\\
U_0&=3\theta_0-2\pi=1.8365085806160\ldots,\\
\lambda_0&=\sin(\theta_0/2)=0.97643694937291\ldots,\\
\phi_0&=2\pi-2\theta_0=0.8700560486492\ldots .
\end{aligned}}
\tag{170.8}
\]

For every physical `sigma<=s_0`, monotonicity gives

\[
\theta\le\theta_0,
\qquad U\le U_0,
\qquad\lambda\le\lambda_0.
\]

Replacing `theta,U,lambda` by these endpoint values decreases the lower bound for `widetilde J`:

- `G` is increasing, so larger `theta` decreases the area term;
- larger `U` increases the negative directional product, whose other factor is nonnegative;
- larger `lambda` increases the nonnegative sparse penalty.

Enlarge the feasible domain to

\[
x+y+z+w\le s_0.
\]

For fixed `x,y,z`, the relaxed lower bound is strictly decreasing in `w`: the area derivative is negative, the sparse penalty increases, and the directional factor `B` increases while `C=U_0-x-y+2z>0` stays fixed. Hence the relaxed minimum fills all unused slack into `w`:

\[
\boxed{x+y+z+w=s_0.}
\tag{170.9}
\]

This is only an optimization relaxation; no fictitious physical source is introduced.

---

## 170.2 Geometry remains in the inherited branch

The hostile-audited largest-gap transfer has derivative above `0.30` on the relevant rectangle, and the coarse `delta>=0.1` branch costs more than `0.0326`. Since

\[
w\le0.0172<0.0326,
\]

that branch remains impossible and

\[
\boxed{\delta\le{10\over3}w.}
\tag{170.10}
\]

The exact complement identity yields

\[
x_2+x_3+\tau=\delta+x
\le x+{10\over3}w
\le {10\over3}s_0
=0.0573333\ldots .
\tag{170.11}
\]

This is tiny compared with the residual principal gap `phi_0>0.8700`. The tangent-lift extent also remains strict:

\[
U_0+{10s_0\over3}
=1.8938419139\ldots<\pi.
\tag{170.12}
\]

The coarser principal-corridor margin is still `0.0949897...>0`, and

\[
Q(s_0)=0.100673979368082\ldots<0.101<\pi/3.
\tag{170.13}
\]

Thus the fourth-gap ordering, tangent lift, circular-segment area identity, tooth inverse branch, and signed-return theorem are all still valid at the enlarged endpoint.

---

## 170.3 Exact correlated simplex

Using (170.9), set

\[
w=s_0-x-y-z.
\]

Then

\[
r={10s_0\over3}-{7x\over3}-{10y\over3}-{10z\over3},
\tag{170.14}
\]

\[
C=U_0-x-y+2z,
\tag{170.15}
\]

\[
B=Q(y)+{10s_0\over3}
-{7x\over3}-{7y\over3}-{10z\over3}.
\tag{170.16}
\]

The relaxed bracket is

\[
\mathcal F(x,y,z)
=A(r)-\lambda_0P(x,y,z)-2CB,
\tag{170.17}
\]

where

\[
A(r)={\pi\over4}-G(\theta_0)-2G(\phi_8+r)-G(r),
\]

and

\[
P=10z+{x\over2}+{25w\over3}+{z\over\kappa}.
\]

The correlation now excludes the incompatible independent maxima.

---

## 170.4 The global minimizer has `x=z=0`

Let

\[
A_r=-2G'(\phi_8+r)-G'(r)<0.
\]

Direct differentiation gives

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal F_x
=-{7\over3}A_r
+{47\over6}\lambda_0
+2B+{14\over3}C>0.
}
\tag{170.18}
\]

Every term is positive, so every minimizer has

\[
\boxed{x=0.}
\tag{170.19}
\]

Similarly,

\[
\mathcal F_z
=-{10\over3}A_r
-\lambda_0\left(10+{1\over\kappa}-{25\over3}\right)
-4B+{20\over3}C.
\tag{170.20}
\]

Throughout the simplex,

\[
C\ge U_0-s_0=1.8193085806\ldots,
\]

and, using the original nonnegative expression for `B`,

\[
B\le Q(s_0)+{10s_0\over3}<0.158008.
\]

Dropping the favorable area derivative still gives

\[
\boxed{\mathcal F_z>6.22.}
\tag{170.21}
\]

Thus every minimizer also has

\[
\boxed{z=0.}
\tag{170.22}
\]

This is the decisive improvement: the slack location producing the worst `z` penalty cannot coexist with the directional-product extremizer.

---

## 170.5 One-dimensional strictly convex envelope

With `x=z=0`, put

\[
w=s_0-y,
\qquad
r={10\over3}(s_0-y).
\]

The exact relaxed lower bound becomes

\[
\begin{aligned}
H(y)={}&{\pi\over4}-G(\theta_0)
-2G\left(\phi_8+{10\over3}(s_0-y)\right)
-G\left({10\over3}(s_0-y)\right)\\
&-{25\lambda_0\over3}(s_0-y)\\
&-2(U_0-y)
\left(Q(y)+{10s_0\over3}-{7y\over3}\right),
\end{aligned}
\tag{170.23}
\]

for `0<=y<=s_0`.

For

\[
F(q)=\sin q+\sqrt3(1-\cos q)-q,
\]

implicit differentiation of `F(Q(y))=y/2` gives

\[
Q'={1\over2F'(Q)},
\qquad
Q''=-{F''(Q)\over4F'(Q)^3}<0.
\tag{170.24}
\]

Since `Q<0.101`,

\[
F'(Q)<0.175,
\qquad
F''(Q)>1.62,
\]

so

\[
-Q''>75.7,
\qquad
Q'>2.857.
\tag{170.25}
\]

The second derivative of the area term is bounded below by `-25/6`. Direct differentiation of the product term therefore yields the uniform bound

\[
\boxed{
H''(y)
>-{25\over6}
+4\left({1\over0.35}-{7\over3}\right)
+2(1.8193)(75.7)
>273.
}
\tag{170.26}
\]

Hence `H` is strictly convex and has a unique minimum.

Direct endpoint-safe evaluation gives

\[
H'(0.00669)<-0.0068,
\qquad
H'(0.00670)>0.0059.
\tag{170.27}
\]

Thus the unique minimizer lies in this interval. At its midpoint,

\[
H(0.006695)
=0.0023269894\ldots .
\tag{170.28}
\]

Within the bracket, moving at most `5e-6` from the midpoint can lower the value by less than `3.5e-8`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\min_{0\le y\le s_0}H(y)>0.0023269>0.00232.
}
\tag{170.29}
\]

Consequently `widetilde J>0` throughout the full relaxed endpoint simplex, contradicting the necessary signed-return inequality.

### Theorem 170.A — fully correlated sparse-return exclusion

No proof-only full-core Type-II survivor in the inherited sparse/directional signed-return scope can satisfy

\[
\boxed{0\le\sigma\le0.0172.}
\tag{170.30}
\]

