## 170.6 Envelope and hostile-audit disposition

Solving the same reduced one-dimensional problem as a function of endpoint slack gives first numerical zero

\[
0.0173116294\ldots .
\]

No theorem is claimed at that root. The safe endpoint `0.0172` is more than `1.1e-4` below it and retains a fixed `>0.00232` return margin.

The independent hostile audit rechecked:

- every endpoint relaxation changes `widetilde J` in the conservative direction;
- filling unused slack into `w` strictly decreases the relaxed lower bound;
- the `delta>=0.1` branch remains excluded;
- fourth-gap, tangent-lift, corridor, and tooth-inverse geometry remains strict;
- `mathcal F_x>0` identically and `mathcal F_z>6.22` uniformly;
- `H''>273` globally and the unique minimum bracket has fixed positive margin;
- no source point is deleted and no contact-isolated coordinate enters a core affine-kernel theorem.

The earlier §167 `sigma<=0.0105` theorem is strictly superseded as a full-core window bound. The surviving sparse/directional full-core regime now has

\[
\boxed{\sigma>0.0172,}
\]

corresponding at the endpoint to wrapping-carrier diameter parameter

\[
\lambda>0.9764369493729\ldots .
\]

Further decimal optimization of this same scalar mechanism has little room; the next useful step should exploit the geometry of the remaining larger-slack, nearly diameter-spanning carrier.

The Type-I inactive-container branch remains open.

**Disposition:** PASS at audited-working proof-only scope. C-II and Level I remain open.